When a split is made, the tree calculates:
The weighted Gini index of the child nodes (after the split), and subtracts this value from the original (parent) Gini to compute the Gini gain.
The feature selected must be the one that provides the highest Gini gain, the highest information gain, the feature that best reduce class mixing (impurity) in the resulting branches.

